\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Schematic and fitted components of the attractor model.} \textbf  {a}, Schematic illustration of the shared fitted landscape. Gaussian-component widths follow the fitted power law, while widths and depths are floored for visibility; the orange $1/n$ curve illustrates the power-law decline in number-word frequency that motivates widening and is not an empirical frequency series. Small numbers therefore appear as deep, isolated components and larger numbers as broad, overlapping components. Figure~\ref {fig:comparison}c reports the width index $n_{\mathrm  {width}}$. \textbf  {b}, Fitted attractor clean-up map from the initial estimate $y_0$ to the bounded final state $x^*(y_0)$. The light-grey diagonal is the matched static readout ($x^*=y_0$); the black curve is the transformation produced by clean-up under the fitted dynamics. Insets show the resulting distributions over integer responses, $p(r\mid x^*)$, for $y_0=2,6,13$. \textbf  {c}, The selected response-time link, $\mathrm  {RT} = t_0 + \tau \,E[T]$, evaluated with the 160-ms sensory parameters and decomposed into basal latency and convergence time. An extended integer support ($1$--$30$) is used in this panel to avoid edge truncation near the upper displayed numerosities.}}{3}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:conceptual}{{1}{3}{\textbf {Schematic and fitted components of the attractor model.} \textbf {a}, Schematic illustration of the shared fitted landscape. Gaussian-component widths follow the fitted power law, while widths and depths are floored for visibility; the orange $1/n$ curve illustrates the power-law decline in number-word frequency that motivates widening and is not an empirical frequency series. Small numbers therefore appear as deep, isolated components and larger numbers as broad, overlapping components. Figure~\ref {fig:comparison}c reports the width index $n_{\mathrm {width}}$. \textbf {b}, Fitted attractor clean-up map from the initial estimate $y_0$ to the bounded final state $x^*(y_0)$. The light-grey diagonal is the matched static readout ($x^*=y_0$); the black curve is the transformation produced by clean-up under the fitted dynamics. Insets show the resulting distributions over integer responses, $p(r\mid x^*)$, for $y_0=2,6,13$. \textbf {c}, The selected response-time link, $\mathrm {RT} = t_0 + \tau \,E[T]$, evaluated with the 160-ms sensory parameters and decomposed into basal latency and convergence time. An extended integer support ($1$--$30$) is used in this panel to avoid edge truncation near the upper displayed numerosities}{figure.caption.4}{}}
