\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Model comparison and the link between landscape width and estimation acuity.} \textbf  {a}, Categorical-response channel: $\Delta $AIC of each model relative to the winning power-law attractor with clean-up, over the ${\sim }53{,}000$ individual responses. Bars are coloured by model role: attractor with clean-up, matched static readout of the same wells, or external benchmark. Turning clean-up off, while retaining the same landscape and sensory parameterization but requiring no stopping-budget worsens the fit, and constant-width wells ($p=0$, $\sigma _n=\sigma _c$) worsen it further. The purely driven attractor (``Power-law wells + driven accumulator'', see Methods), whose input is the still-accumulating count rather than a completed estimate, fits far worse, because a partial count captures the wrong integer (\hyperref  [sup-sm:comparison]{Supplementary Material~2}). Weber/ANS and the bounded-optimal model of~\citet  {cheyette_unified_2020} (``Normative (C\&P)'') are shown for reference. \textbf  {b}, Response-time channel: $\Delta $AIC of the three RT links on the winning landscape, with one mapping shared across durations. Convergence time alone and the hybrid link perform almost identically, whereas entropy alone performs substantially worse. \textbf  {c}, Individual differences in the 360 sessions completing all five durations. Non-overlapping numerosity ranges informed landscape resolution ($n\leq 8$) and Weber noise ($n\geq 9$). Points show duration-averaged empirical-Bayes estimates; the inset reports their latent correlation conditional on the participant lapse effect. \textbf  {d}, Effective Weber fraction: the coefficient of variation of the fitted response distribution per numerosity and exposure duration. Solid lines are the model, dots the data, and the dotted vertical marks the shared $n_{\mathrm  {width}}$; the coefficient of variation approaches a roughly constant plateau at larger numerosities. }}{5}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:comparison}{{2}{5}{\textbf {Model comparison and the link between landscape width and estimation acuity.} \textbf {a}, Categorical-response channel: $\Delta $AIC of each model relative to the winning power-law attractor with clean-up, over the ${\sim }53{,}000$ individual responses. Bars are coloured by model role: attractor with clean-up, matched static readout of the same wells, or external benchmark. Turning clean-up off, while retaining the same landscape and sensory parameterization but requiring no stopping-budget worsens the fit, and constant-width wells ($p=0$, $\sigma _n=\sigma _c$) worsen it further. The purely driven attractor (``Power-law wells + driven accumulator'', see Methods), whose input is the still-accumulating count rather than a completed estimate, fits far worse, because a partial count captures the wrong integer (\hyperref [sup-sm:comparison]{Supplementary Material~2}). Weber/ANS and the bounded-optimal model of~\citet {cheyette_unified_2020} (``Normative (C\&P)'') are shown for reference. \textbf {b}, Response-time channel: $\Delta $AIC of the three RT links on the winning landscape, with one mapping shared across durations. Convergence time alone and the hybrid link perform almost identically, whereas entropy alone performs substantially worse. \textbf {c}, Individual differences in the 360 sessions completing all five durations. Non-overlapping numerosity ranges informed landscape resolution ($n\leq 8$) and Weber noise ($n\geq 9$). Points show duration-averaged empirical-Bayes estimates; the inset reports their latent correlation conditional on the participant lapse effect. \textbf {d}, Effective Weber fraction: the coefficient of variation of the fitted response distribution per numerosity and exposure duration. Solid lines are the model, dots the data, and the dotted vertical marks the shared $n_{\mathrm {width}}$; the coefficient of variation approaches a roughly constant plateau at larger numerosities}{figure.caption.6}{}}
